<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04QV">Notation <span data-tag="04QV">10.1.1.5</span>.</a> Let $X_{\bullet }$ be a simplicial object of an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="02JH" href="/tag/02JH">7.1.1.13</a> that, if $X_{\bullet }$ admits a geometric realization $X$, then the isomorphism class of $X$ is uniquely determined. To emphasize this, we will often denote $X$ by $| X_{\bullet } |$ and refer to it as <em>the</em> geometric realization of $X_{\bullet }$. Beware that, in the case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is (the nerve of) the category of sets, this is incompatible with the convention of Notation <a data-tag="0020" href="/tag/0020">1.2.3.3</a> (see Warning <a data-tag="04QU" href="/tag/04QU">10.1.1.4</a>). </p>
</article>
